<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revolt of the Comuneros</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1520">1520</a>–<a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as War of the Communities of Castile</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Revolt of the Comuneros was a rising of Castilian towns in 1520–1521 against the new king Charles I, his Flemish advisers and his tax demands after his election as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and a junta of cities, it was crushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-villalar">Battle of Villalar</a> in April 1521. Afterwards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-castile">Cortes of Castile</a> rarely resisted royal taxation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-comuneros.jpg" alt="Revolt of the Comuneros" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Gisbert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comuneros.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of Queen Isabella I in 1504 the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> passed to her daughter Joanna, who was confined because of her mental instability. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> ruled as regent until his death in 1516, when Joanna's sixteen-year-old son Charles became co-monarch. Raised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> with little Castilian, he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in October 1517 with a large retinue of Flemish nobles and clerics, which bred mistrust among Castile's social elites.</p><p>Charles was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> in 1519, after more than a million florins had been spent on bribes. To cover the debt he convened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in late March 1520 and tried to fill it with representatives he could bribe. When he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1520 he put the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> cardinal Adrian of Utrecht in charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In April 1520 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> rose. A crowd drove out the royal administrators and elected a committee under Juan López de Padilla and Pedro Laso de la Vega, calling itself a Comunidad. Segovia, led by the noble Juan Bravo, held out against the royal investigator Rodrigo Ronquillo in June. A revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a>, the Santa Junta, met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a>, and Padilla became its Captain-General. When royal troops under Antonio de Fonseca tried on 21 August to seize artillery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a>, a fire destroyed much of the town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> and other cities then declared for the rebels, and Adrian fled.</p><p>The comuneros moved to Tordesillas on 29 August to proclaim Joanna sole sovereign, and thirteen cities sent representatives. Joanna, however, refused to sign their edicts. Risings against the landed nobility alarmed the aristocrats, who turned against the rebels, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> left the Junta in October. The royalists took Tordesillas. On 23 April 1521 their army defeated the comuneros at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-villalar">Battle of Villalar</a>, and Padilla, Bravo and Francisco Maldonado were beheaded the next day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> held out under María Pacheco until October 1521.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>María Pacheco escaped to Portugal and lived there in exile for her remaining ten years. Bishop Antonio de Acuña of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> was executed after killing a guard while trying to escape. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 16 July 1522 and, because many important people had supported the rebels or been slow to declare for him, pressed repression only sporadically. Historians disagree about the revolt. Some see one of the first modern revolutions, others a rebellion against high taxes and foreign control. From the 19th century Spanish liberals mythologised it, and 23 April is now observed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León Day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Revolt of the Comuneros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its towns rose against the new king's foreign advisers and tax demands in the <b>Revolt of the Comuneros</b>, crushed at the Battle of Villalar in 1521, after which the Cortes offered little resistance to royal taxation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
